---
import { apiBook } from "@intentic-dev/site-content/api";
import DocsLayout from "../../components/DocsLayout.astro";
import DocsRelated from "../../components/DocsRelated.astro";

/* THE VERIFY STEP.
 *
 * It was four paragraphs at the foot of the publishing guide, under a heading about registries, and it is read by
 * two people who never open that guide: an author deciding whether "verified" is worth pursuing, and a user
 * deciding whether to install. Searching the docs for "verified" returned the gallery and nothing else.
 *
 * THE PAGE'S ONE JOB is to be exact about who is claiming what. Three different parties make three different
 * promises here — a commit sha, the manifest gate, and a human reviewer — and the failure mode this page exists
 * to prevent is a reader collapsing them into one feeling of safety. So every row below says what it does NOT
 * guarantee, in the same breath. */

const states = [
    {
        state: `listed`,
        claim: `The pointer resolves, the manifest parses, the publisher owns the source repo.`,
        read: `Nobody read the code.`,
    },
    {
        state: `verified`,
        claim: `Somebody here read the source at that exact commit.`,
        read: `A newer commit is a new listing, and is unverified until read again.`,
    },
    {
        state: `blocked`,
        claim: `Known-malicious or known-broken, with the reason recorded alongside.`,
        read: `The row stays in the file rather than being deleted.`,
    },
];

const layers = [
    {
        layer: `Sha pinning`,
        guarantees: `The approved code is the running code. Force-pushing your branch changes nothing on an installed sandbox.`,
        doesnt: `That the code is good.`,
    },
    {
        layer: `The manifest gate`,
        guarantees: `Blast radius. Undeclared registrations are refused; undeclared routes throw; secrets never reach the bundle.`,
        doesnt: `That declared powers are used well.`,
    },
    {
        layer: `Registry review`,
        guarantees: `The pointer resolves, the publisher is real, the description matches what the manifest contributes.`,
        doesnt: `A code audit. It is not one.`,
    },
];

const checks = [
    { field: `manifest`, what: `The manifest at that commit parses, or the reason it doesn't, verbatim.` },
    { field: `bundle`, what: `The entry bundle exists and can load where it would be installed — or "none", for an extension that ships no UI.` },
    { field: `engines`, what: `The app version range the manifest declares. A raw fact, not a verdict: the scan doesn't know which version you run.` },
];
---

<DocsLayout
    book={apiBook}
    pageId="verify"
    heading="Verification & trust"
    lead="A listing carries one of three words, and each is a different party making a different promise. This page is about exactly what each one claims — and, just as importantly, what none of them claims."
>
    <h2>The three words a listing can carry</h2>
    <p>
        Every entry in a registry has a <code>trust</code> state. It is asserted by the pull request that lists you, which means a human wrote
        it down and another human reviewed it — there is no automated promotion, in either direction.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>State</th><th>What it claims</th><th>What it doesn't</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                states.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td><code>{row.state}</code></td>
                        <td>{row.claim}</td>
                        <td>{row.read}</td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        <strong>Listed is the honest default</strong>, and most rows will always be listed. An entry that states no trust level at all — on a
        third-party registry that never adopted the field — resolves to listed, because a registry that doesn't use it hasn't asserted
        anything and shouldn't be read as though it had.
    </p>
    <p>
        A blocked entry <em>stays in the file</em>. Removing the row would hide it from people browsing and tell the people who already
        installed it nothing, which is backwards: they are the ones at risk. Every blocked row carries its reason, so an advisory can quote it
        rather than saying "this was withdrawn" and leaving the reader to guess.
    </p>

    <h2>What the nightly scan checks</h2>
    <p>
        Separate from all of that, and colder: a job re-derives a handful of facts from your pinned commit, as a stranger holding nothing but
        the pointer. It answers the browser's one question — <em>will this thing load?</em> — and it is why a gallery card can show a
        <strong>✓ loads</strong> mark that nobody typed.
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Checked</th><th>What comes back</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {checks.map((row) => (<tr><td><code>{row.field}</code></td><td>{row.what}</td></tr>))}
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        Each answer is bound to the sha it was read from. Repoint your listing at a new commit and the checks simply <strong>disappear</strong>
        from the card until the next scan, rather than yesterday's verdict describing today's code. An absent check says nothing at all, which
        is the honest thing for it to say.
    </p>
    <p>
        This is evidence, not endorsement. "It loads" and "somebody read it" are different claims made by different parties, and the gallery
        keeps them visually apart for that reason.
    </p>

    <h2>Three layers, and precisely what each guarantees</h2>
    <p>
        Trust in an extension is not one thing. Three separate mechanisms protect the person installing yours, they fail in different ways, and
        the reason to lay them out in a table is that the middle column is easy to over-read:
    </p>
    <table>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it guarantees</th><th>What it doesn't</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            {
                layers.map((row) => (
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{row.layer}</strong></td>
                        <td>{row.guarantees}</td>
                        <td>{row.doesnt}</td>
                    </tr>
                ))
            }
        </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>
        The honest summary: installing an extension is trusting its author, the same way installing an editor plugin is. What intentic
        contributes is that the trust is <em>bounded and legible</em> — the owner can read what your extension may touch before saying yes, and
        it cannot grow past that without asking them again.
    </p>

    <h2>Getting verified</h2>
    <p>
        Ask, in the pull request that lists you or one after it. Verification is somebody reading your source at the commit you pinned, so what
        makes it quick is what makes it possible to read: a small entry bundle you didn't minify beyond recognition, a manifest whose declared
        routes are the ones the code actually calls, and a description that matches the two.
    </p>
    <p>Three things follow from it, and they're worth knowing before you decide it matters to you:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>The gallery ranks verified first</strong>, and sorts by stars within that. It is the one editorial thumb on the scale.</li>
        <li>
            <strong>It is a precondition for unattended updates.</strong> An owner may set an extension to update itself, but that policy only
            fires when four things hold at once, and a human-verified listing is the first of them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>It expires at every new commit.</strong> Verified describes a commit, not a publisher. Ship a new sha and the listing is
            back to what a fresh reading of it would support, which is the only way the word can keep meaning anything.
        </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When a listing goes bad</h2>
    <p>
        The same daily comparison that shows an owner "update available" also carries trust changes back to sandboxes that already installed
        you. Two of them do something:
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>Blocked</strong> raises an advisory on the installed row, quoting the registry's recorded reason, and by default switches
            the extension off. That is the one automatic action the model permits — disabling runs no new code and reverses with a click.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Security fix</strong> is the mirror image: a flag on your new entry saying its commit fixes a security problem in earlier
            ones. It promotes the update badge from ambient to loud, because there the <em>old</em> version is the dangerous one and waiting is
            the risky choice.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
        Both are asserted by pull request, exactly like <code>trust</code>, and are worth exactly that review. Neither can be set by the code
        being described.
    </p>

    <DocsRelated
        links={[
            { href: "/api/publish/", title: "Publish & registries", why: "get the pointer listed in the first place." },
            { href: "/docs/extensions/", title: "Install & manage extensions", why: "what these words look like at the moment somebody clicks install." },
            { href: "/extensions/", title: "The gallery", why: "the states above, rendered, on real listings." },
        ]}
    />
</DocsLayout>
